#limitation(title: [The "Non-POTS Dysautonomia" Recognition Claim Is Consensus-and-Single-Center, Not Validated])[

The claim that the POTS HR-rise threshold is too restrictive and misses a "non-POTS dysautonomia" population rests on a modified-Delphi expert consensus (Sivakoti et al. 2026), two single-center retrospective registries (Parsaik 2013, Mayo n=184; Boris 2020, CHOP), and a JAMA review with no original data (Chung and Raj 2026) @Sivakoti2026POTSConsensus @Parsaik2013OInoTachycardia @Boris2020HRnotPredictive. No prospective multi-center study validates the subthreshold construct or a threshold-free classification.

Two balancing concerns limit how strongly the recognition claim should be read:

- *Over-diagnosis and dilution risk (C10.1):* Broadening recognition to all "subthreshold dysautonomia" could label normal orthostatic variation or deconditioning as disease, and dilute the specificity the $gt.eq 30$ bpm threshold enforces by separating POTS from inappropriate sinus tachycardia and anxiety-related tachycardia. The over-diagnosis risk was screened in the literature search and returned no empirical support — it is asserted, not yet demonstrated.

- *Null-hypothesis (C11.1):* No study shows that recognizing or reclassifying subthreshold patients changes outcomes. The label may carry no prognostic or therapeutic meaning beyond symptom management; Parsaik 2013 demonstrates shared biology but not a shared trajectory.

These limits mean the recognition-construct conclusion is appropriately integrated here as a `#clinical-finding` with moderate certainty — not as a high-certainty mechanism, and not as grounds to abandon the HR threshold in clinical practice.

*Certainty:* 0.30 (methodological/balancing assessment; applies to the recognition claim's strength).

*Consequence:* Until a prospective multi-center study demonstrates that identifying subthreshold dysautonomia changes outcomes, clinicians should recognize the possibility without loosening diagnostic discipline — the missing-patient risk and the over-diagnosis risk are both real and unquantified.

] <lim:pots-threshold-recognition-limits>
